const projects = [
  {
    heading: "ADO HTML Commit Previewer",
    paragraphs: [
      "Azure DevOps' built-in preview for HTML files changed in a commit or PR has no CSS and never resolves repo-relative image paths, so reviewing exported documentation before merge was effectively impossible. I built a browser extension that detects commit and PR pages, lists every changed HTML file, and renders each one with real images and consistent styling.",
      "The harder problem was authentication without a token: the extension proxies API calls through a content script running in the user's own Azure DevOps tab, so requests carry real session cookies, sidestepping the SameSite restrictions that block a direct cross-origin fetch from the extension's service worker.",
      "Every previewed page is attacker-controlled input — a committed file, not trusted content — so rendering goes through layered defenses: script and style stripping, a strict injected CSP, external-resource blocking so a file can't beacon out to detect it was previewed, and a sandboxed iframe with no script execution as the final backstop against any sanitizer gap, including a known mutation-XSS risk from re-serializing parsed HTML.",
      "One manifest.json runs on Chrome, Edge, and Firefox, handling the real differences between Chrome's service-worker background model and Firefox's background-scripts model, verified against Mozilla's own web-ext linter. 45 unit tests, using Node's built-in test runner and jsdom, cover the security-critical sanitization logic directly.",
