    <main class="wrap">
      <section class="doc-section hero">
        <div class="content">
          <h1 class="gradient-text">Anthony Mutisya</h1>
          <p class="role">Documentation Engineer</p>
          <p class="role-sub">Technical Writing Lead at Advantive</p>
          <p class="tagline">
            Most engineering notes aren't wrong, just unreadable by the
            people who need them. I turn them into documentation that is.
          </p>
          <DiffPanels examples={diffExamples} />
          <p class="hero-links">
            <a href="#contact">Get in touch</a> <a href="#experience">See my experience</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <Section id="overview">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>
          Six years, four industries: ERP, manufacturing execution,
          enterprise sales, and medical software. The product changes every
          time. What doesn't change is the gap between what engineering
          built and what a user needs to understand to use it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Closing that gap is the job. Sometimes it's a forty-page user
          guide. Sometimes it's one error message. Lately it's meant
          rebuilding the systems that produce the documentation in the first
          place — structured authoring in MadCap Flare, metadata and
          conditional-text patterns, single-sourcing so one source feeds
          every output instead of five people editing five copies.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also have a QA background, which matters more than it sounds
          like it should. Bad documentation usually isn't badly written —
          it's just wrong, because nobody checked it against what the
          product actually does. I check.
        </p>
      </Section>

      <Section id="principles">
